      "inputTokens": 145,
      "outputTokens": 1071,
      "costUsd": 0.0097115,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here. The integral of sine x, from zero to two pi, is zero, because the arch below the axis cancels the arch above. Picture sine t as the velocity of a lift, in floors per second, over two pi seconds. For the first half, it rises two floors. For the second half, it comes back down two. It ends where it started, so its net change is zero. But it still travelled four floors, and the shaded region has area four, two for each arch. The integral keeps signs, and the area doesn't. So how do signed integrals differ from total area, and how do we find the area when part of the region is below the axis?"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-i10/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 968,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 0.9029999999999969,
        "after": "area.",
        "before": "Then"
