      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 177,
      "outputTokens": 1184,
      "costUsd": 0.0107445,
      "pronunciationText": "The same method works for every function in the course. Take y equals e to the x, minus two, from zero to one. It meets the axis where e to the x is two, so x is the natural log of two, about zero point six nine. At zero, y is minus one, so the first piece is below the axis. At one, y is e minus two, which is positive. With the primitive e to the x minus two x, the first piece is one, minus two times the natural log of two, which is negative. The second is e minus four, plus two times the natural log of two. Make the first one positive and add. The area is e minus five, plus four times the natural log of two, about zero point four nine. Keep the log exact all the way through, and round only at the end."
    },
